.TH MU2EDAQ-CHECKS.YAML 5 "2026-09-17" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
checks.yaml \- health-check thresholds, expectations and profiles
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I config/checks.yaml
.SH DESCRIPTION
Says what "healthy" means: the thresholds a check compares against, the
filesystems and services a node is expected to have, and which checks apply to
which class of host.
.PP
The semantics were taken from
.I mu2edaq-operations/scripts/check_functions.sh
so that a node this tool calls healthy is also healthy by the on-node script's
standard.
.PP
A check id listed here with no implementation is reported as
.B skip
with an explanation, never silently dropped: an unimplemented check must not
look like a passing one.
.BR "mu2e-power-recovery \-\-list\-checks"
prints what is actually implemented.
.SH SECTIONS
.SS thresholds
.TP
.IR disk_full_pct ", " disk_warn_pct
Filesystem usage that fails and warns. Defaults 90 and 80; 90 matches the
on-node script.
.TP
.I data_link_speed_mbps
Expected data-network link rate, from
.IR /sys/class/net/<nic>/speed .
Default 10000. A link that renegotiated at 1 Gb/s after a switch reboot is a
real and quiet failure: everything works, ten times too slowly.
.TP
.I load_per_core_warn
Load average per core above which a freshly booted node is suspicious.
.TP
.I max_post_boot_uptime_s
Uptime above which a node that phase 2 just powered on is flagged \(em it did
not actually reboot, so the IPMI command probably reached a different chassis.
.TP
.IR ping_count ", " ping_timeout_s ", " ssh_connect_timeout_s ", " ipmi_timeout_s
Per-probe budgets.
.SS mounts
.TP
.I common
Filesystems every node should have.
.TP
.IR mc2 ", " mc1 ", " teststand
Additional per-location expectations.
.TP
.I paths
Plain existence checks, which catch a half-mounted tree that
.BR mountpoint (1)
alone would pass.
.PP
A mount entry may name a
.IR server .
The check is then skipped on that host \(em
.I mu2e-mgr-01
does not NFS-mount its own
.I /home
\(em and, in the
.I disk.nfs_from_mgr
check, the actual mount source is compared against it. That is not the same
test as
.IR disk.mounts :
a stale entry can leave
.I /home
mounted from an address that no longer serves it, which passes
.B mountpoint
and fails the moment anything reads a file.
.SS services
Daemons whose absence means the node is up but not usable, listed under
.I common
and per host class.
.SS profiles
Check ids per host class.
.I base
is merged into every other profile and comes first, because its members \(em
reachability and login \(em are the ones whose failure explains every later
failure.
.PP
The shipped profiles are
.BR base ", " gateway ", " manager ", " datalogger ", " dcs ", " cfo ,
.BR readout ", " teststand " and " other .
A host whose class has no profile uses
.BR other .
.SS mesh
Phase 3 settings.
.TP
.I networks
Each entry gives a network
.IR name ,
whether to probe a
.I full_mesh
or sample against anchors, and whether to run an
.IR mtu_probe .
The data network is full-mesh with an MTU probe; the lab and IPMI networks are
sampled, which is O(N) rather than O(N^2).
.TP
.I anchors
Representative targets used when
.I full_mesh
is false.
.TP
.I mtu_payload_bytes
ICMP payload for the jumbo-frame probe. 8972 plus an 8-byte ICMP header plus a
20-byte IP header is exactly a 9000-byte frame, sent with DF set, so it fails
if any hop on the path is not jumbo-clean.
.TP
.IR count ", " timeout_s
Per-pair probe budget.
.SH EXAMPLES
Warn earlier about disk usage:
.RS
.EX
thresholds:
  disk_warn_pct: 70
.EE
.RE
.PP
Require a new daemon on the data loggers:
.RS
.EX
services:
  datalogger:
    \- node_exporter
    \- mu2e_fts
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the implemented checks and what each verifies:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-recovery \-\-list\-checks
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
.BR mu2e-power-netcheck (1),
.BR mu2edaq-power-recovery (7)
.SH AUTHOR
A. Norman <anorman@fnal.gov>, Mu2e DAQ Operations.
